Below is a **complete, copy-and-paste ready** example that shows how to:

1. **Add a button** to the page (you can also use an existing UI element).  
2. **Listen for the click** and call `map.flyTo()` (or `map.easeTo()` if you want a custom easing curve).  
3. Keep the transition **smooth and accessible** – set a reasonable duration, use an easing function, and optionally animate the pitch/bearing so the user can stay oriented.

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
  <meta charset="UTF-8">
  <title>MapLibre – smooth flyTo on button click</title>
  <style>
    body, html { margin:0; height:100%; }
    #map { position:absolute; top:0; bottom:0; width:100%; }
    /* Simple button styling – keep it reachable by keyboard */
    #flyBtn {
      position: absolute;
      top: 10px; left: 10px;
      padding: 0.5rem 1rem;
      background: #fff;
      border: 1px solid #999;
      border-radius: 4px;
      cursor: pointer;
      font-family: sans-serif;
    }
    #flyBtn:focus { outline: 2px solid #0066ff; }
  </style>

  <!-- MapLibre GL JS (use the latest version you have) -->
  <script src="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.4.0/dist/maplibre-gl.js"></script>
  <link href="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.4.0/dist/maplibre-gl.css" rel="stylesheet"/>
</head>

<body>
<div id="map"></div>
<button id="flyBtn">Go to Paris</button>

<script>
  // -------------------------------------------------
  // 1️⃣ Initialise the map (replace with your own style)
  // -------------------------------------------------
  const map = new maplibregl.Map({
    container: 'map',
    style: 'https://demotiles.maplibre.org/style.json', // light vector basemap
    center: [-74.006, 40.7128], // New York City
    zoom: 10,
    pitch: 0,
    bearing: 0
  });

  // -------------------------------------------------
  // 2️⃣ Define the target camera position
  // -------------------------------------------------
  const target = {
    // Longitude, latitude of the new centre
    center: [2.3522, 48.8566], // Paris, France
    // Optional: zoom, pitch, bearing – adjust to your design
    zoom: 12,
    pitch: 45,          // tilt the view a bit for a 3-D feel
    bearing: 30,        // rotate the map clockwise 30°
    // Duration in ms (default 1000). 1500-2000 feels “smooth” for most UI.
    duration: 1500,
    // Easing function – MapLibre ships with a few helpers.
    // `maplibregl.easeOut` is a good default (starts fast, slows down).
    easing: maplibregl.easeOut,
    // If you want the map to keep the user-controlled “dragging” ability
    // during the animation, set `animate: true` (default).  Setting it to
    // false would snap instantly after the animation finishes.
    animate: true
  };

  // -------------------------------------------------
  // 3️⃣ Hook the button click → flyTo
  // -------------------------------------------------
  const btn = document.getElementById('flyBtn');
  btn.addEventListener('click', () => {
    // `flyTo` automatically adds a smooth “flight” curve (zoom-in/out + pan).
    // If you need a custom curve you can call `easeTo` with the same options.
    map.flyTo(target);
  });

  // -------------------------------------------------
  // 4️⃣ (Optional) Keep the button focusable for accessibility
  // -------------------------------------------------
  // The button already receives focus via the browser tab order.
  // If you add more UI, remember to give each interactive element a
  // visible focus style (see the CSS above) and an ARIA label if the
  // text isn’t descriptive enough.
</script>
</body>
</html>
```

---

## Why `flyTo` (or `easeTo`)?

| Feature | `flyTo` | `easeTo` |
|---------|---------|----------|
| **Default “flight” curve** (zoom-out, pan, zoom-in) | ✅ | ❌ (linear) |
| **Custom easing** (`maplibregl.easeInOut`, `easeOut`, etc.) | ✅ (via `easing`) | ✅ |
| **Control over pitch & bearing** | ✅ | ✅ |
| **Stops user interaction** while animating (default) | ✅ (`animate: true`) | ✅ |
| **Can be cancelled** (`map.stop()`) | ✅ | ✅ |

If you want a **pure linear pan/zoom** (no “flight” arc), replace `map.flyTo(target)` with:

```js
map.easeTo({
  ...target,
  // remove the default fly-to curve by using a linear easing
  easing: t => t   // identity function = linear
});
```

---

## Tips for a “smooth” experience

| Tip | Reason |
|-----|--------|
| **Duration 1200-1800 ms** | Long enough to be perceived as a transition, short enough not to feel sluggish. |
| **Easing `easeOut` or `easeInOut`** | Gives a natural acceleration/deceleration; `easeOut` feels like a “fly-by”. |
| **Don’t change the style while animating** | Switching basemap tiles or toggling layers mid-flight can cause a jarring visual jump. |
| **Keep pitch ≤ 60°** | Higher pitch can make the map feel “tilted too far” and may hide labels. |
| **Maintain label readability** – if you’re flying over an imagery basemap, keep the halo settings from the *maplibre-cartography* skill (white text + dark semi-transparent halo). |
| **Keyboard accessibility** – ensure the button is reachable via `Tab` and has a clear focus outline (see CSS). |
| **Screen-reader hint** – if the button triggers a major context change, add `aria-live="polite"` to a hidden status element that announces the new location. |

```html
<div id="status" aria-live="polite" style="position:absolute; left:-9999px;"></div>
<script>
  btn.addEventListener('click', () => {
    map.flyTo(target);
    document.getElementById('status').textContent = 'Flying to Paris, France';
  });
</script>
```

---

## Quick checklist before you ship

1. **Label colors** – If your basemap is satellite imagery, make sure any custom labels you add (or the default basemap labels) use the recommended halo (`rgba(0,0,0,0.75)`) and white text.  
2. **Layer order** – If you inject extra data layers (e.g., a polygon overlay for Paris), add them **before the first symbol layer** so they don’t cover existing place-name labels.  
3. **Contrast** – Verify the combined text-halo contrast meets WCAG 2.1 AA (≥ 4.5:1).  
4. **Performance** – `flyTo` is GPU-accelerated; avoid heavy raster layers with full opacity at low zooms, as they can slow the animation.  

That’s it! Click the button and the map will glide smoothly from New York to Paris, preserving readability and respecting cartographic best practices. Happy mapping!
